import json
from pathlib import Path
R=json.load(open('fit_results.json')); C=json.load(open('clipped_results.json')); W=json.load(open('wheel_results.json'))
Path('models').mkdir(exist_ok=True)
def parameter(n,v,u,description,t='fitted'):
 return {'name':n,'value':float(v),'unit':u,'type':t,'description':description}
Ts=parameter('Ts',.2,'s','Sampling period `Ts = 0.2` s specified in TASK.md; fixed because the samples have a known time spacing.','fixed')
DATA='`data/train.csv`, all three trajectories (`trajectory = 1`, `trajectory = 2`, and `trajectory = 3`), treated as separate experiments. Fit windows start at within-trajectory zero-based rows `0, 5, ..., 120`; each uses the logged initial pose and the next 25 transitions (5 s), without later state correction. Thus 75 windows use inputs through row 144 and target states through row 145. The last four samples are not direct fitting targets because no complete 25-transition window starting on their command-block grid ends there. They are included in the sliding-window validation (starts at every available row through row 124) and in full-trajectory diagnostics. Block-aligned fitting starts reflect the one-second command schedule and reduce near-duplicate starts. No transition crosses trajectory boundaries.'
CRITERION='Minimize the sum of squared open-loop rollout residuals in `x` (m), `y` (m), and heading (rad), over 25 transitions (5 s) per fitting window. The heading residual is the principal circular difference multiplied by a fixed conversion of 0.25 m/rad before squaring. This joint criterion constrains turning as well as translation, avoiding the weak angular identifiability of an `x`-only objective in some orientations. The conversion is an analyst-chosen balance, comparable to the distance traveled over a few seconds, not an estimated sensor-noise variance. No later logged pose corrects a rollout.'
BASEMETHOD='Bounded nonlinear least squares using `scipy.optimize.least_squares`, its default trust-region reflective method, two-point finite-difference Jacobian, linear loss, `ftol = 1e-10`, `xtol = 1e-10`, `gtol = 1e-10`, and `max_nfev = 400`. This directly optimizes the required multi-step simulation rather than differentiating noisy logged poses or minimizing only one-step increments. All final fitted parameters are estimated together in this one rollout-fitting step. The calculation is deterministic and uses no random seed. '
KIN='Discrete-time unicycle/differential-drive kinematics with calibrated command response. A general reference for unicycle kinematics is Siciliano, Sciavicco, Villani and Oriolo, Robotics: Modelling, Planning and Control (Springer, 2009), cited from memory without consulting the book during this round. The supplied task, rather than that reference, defines the robot and sampling period.'
COMMONLIMIT='Only positive forward commands were observed: `0.000034 <= v_f <= 0.298156` m/s, and angular commands satisfy `-0.948148 <= v_a <= 0.941161` rad/s (rounded extrema of `data/train.csv`). Reverse motion and larger commands are not validated. The logs cannot separate true actuator response from odometry distortion. Command-change residuals show lag and within-block correlation, but the allowed state has neither actuator velocity nor previous commands, so this memoryless model cannot reproduce that lag exactly. Long rollouts accumulate error; 30-second training diagnostics are not evidence of 5-second test accuracy. Validation uses only three held-out training trajectories, with overlapping windows; it is not an independent test set and gives no reliable confidence interval.'
INTERPRET='`x`, `y`, and `phi` are the current planar pose, `v_f` and `v_a` are the current forward and angular commands, and `Ts` is the fixed sampling period. `gf` converts commanded forward speed to effective logged forward speed, and `ga` calibrates angular command response. Translation adds a body-forward increment projected onto the world axes. The heading update uses `atan2(sin(phi + Ts*({rate})), cos(phi + Ts*({rate})))` to wrap the angle; wrapping does not alter either translation projection.'
def make(label,result,params,speed,rate,alpha,family,origin,extra,limits,method,script):
 h=f'phi + {alpha}*Ts*({rate})'
 ex={'x_next':f'x + Ts*({speed})*cos({h})','y_next':f'y + Ts*({speed})*sin({h})','phi_next':f'atan2(sin(phi + Ts*({rate})), cos(phi + Ts*({rate})))'}
 desc=INTERPRET.format(rate=rate)+' '+extra+' The complete state-update expressions are `'+ex['x_next']+'`, `'+ex['y_next']+'`, and `'+ex['phi_next']+'`.'
 cv=result['cv_x'];train=result['train']['x']
 limits+=f' On all training sliding windows, `x` rollout RMSE is {train:.6f} m; equal-trajectory pooled leave-one-trajectory-out `x` RMSE is {cv:.6f} m, computed on 25-transition (5 s) windows with one-sample start spacing. These results are diagnostics, not test scores. '+COMMONLIMIT
 obj={'expressions':ex,'parameters':[Ts]+params,'estimation':{'criterion':CRITERION,'data':DATA,'method':method,'script':script},'discussion':{'family':family,'interpretation':desc,'origin':origin,'limitations':limits}}
 Path(f'models/{label}.json').write_text(json.dumps(obj,indent=2)+'\n')

p=R['midpoint']['parameters']
make('CalibratedMidpoint',R['midpoint'],[parameter('gf',p[0],'-','Gain of `v_f`, jointly estimated by 5-second rollout least squares.'),parameter('ga',p[1],'-','Gain of `v_a`, jointly estimated by the same rollout least squares.')], 'gf*v_f','ga*v_a','0.5', KIN+' The translation uses the explicit midpoint (second-order Runge-Kutta) direction for a constant command within a sample.',
 'The pose projections `cos(phi)` and `sin(phi)` and additive heading dynamics come from differential-drive physical reasoning. I tested Euler, midpoint and exact constant-command arc integration. I retained only midpoint in the pool because their 5-second validation errors were almost identical and they are the same structural family. `gf*v_f` and `ga*v_a` were the simplest calibrations consistent with the observed reduced speeds. The fixed half-step direction `phi + 0.5*Ts*ga*v_a` follows numerical integration, not symbolic search.',
 '`gf*v_f` is effective forward speed and `ga*v_a` is effective angular speed. The midpoint direction is `phi + 0.5*Ts*ga*v_a`. There is no assumed drift at zero command and no spatially varying coefficient.',
 'Constant gains average the command-response nonlinearities and trajectory-dependent changes visible in the training data. Midpoint translation omits the exact arc chord factor, negligible for the observed per-sample turn angles.',
 BASEMETHOD+'Starting values were `gf = 0.83` and `ga = 0.68`, rounded from preliminary steady-command regressions on the training data. Bounds were `0.2 <= gf <= 1.5` and `0.1 <= ga <= 1.5`, broad positive gain bounds chosen to avoid implausible reversed command response.', 'fit_models.py')

p=C['clipped']['parameters']
make('ForwardDeadzone',C['clipped'],[parameter('gf',p[0],'-','Forward gain outside the forward dead zone.'),parameter('ga',p[1],'-','Linear angular gain.'),parameter('df',p[2],'m/s','Nonnegative forward command dead-zone threshold.'),parameter('ba',p[3],'rad/s','Constant effective angular drift, fitted jointly with both gains and the threshold.')], 'gf*sign(v_f)*Max(Abs(v_f)-df, 0)','ga*v_a + ba','0.5', KIN+' Input dead-zone nonlinearity in forward actuation and affine angular calibration.',
 'An affine input calibration was tested first because the one-step regressions had nonzero intercepts and the 5-second fit preferred a negative forward intercept. Its extrapolation at `v_f = 0` would predict reverse translation. I therefore replaced that forward map with `gf*sign(v_f)*Max(Abs(v_f)-df, 0)`, a guessed dead-zone form motivated by low-command inactivity and preserving zero translation at zero forward command. The angular bias `ba` is retained as an effective calibration correction, not asserted to be physical drift.',
 '`df` suppresses translation for `Abs(v_f) <= df` m/s. Outside that interval `gf*sign(v_f)*Max(Abs(v_f)-df, 0)` is an odd affine response. `ba` adds an orientation-independent increment `Ts*ba` rad per sample; it can absorb mean angular calibration error but does not imply an identified motor mechanism.',
 'The forward threshold is an effective fit, not a separately observed hardware dead band. The constant angular bias predicts turning at zero angular command, and its leave-one-trajectory-out estimates vary. Negative-command symmetry is assumed, not measured.',
 BASEMETHOD+'Starting values were `gf = 0.9`, `ga = 0.71`, `df = 0.008` m/s, and `ba = 0.01` rad/s, rounded from the earlier affine rollout fit to initialize its zero-preserving replacement. Bounds were `0.2 <= gf <= 1.5`, `0.1 <= ga <= 1.5`, `0 <= df <= 0.06` m/s, and `-0.15 <= ba <= 0.15` rad/s. These retain positive gain and a small command threshold without forcing its presence.', 'fit_clipped.py')

p=R['deadzone']['parameters']
make('AngularDeadzone',R['deadzone'],[parameter('gf',p[0],'-','Linear forward command gain.'),parameter('ga',p[1],'-','Angular gain outside the angular dead zone.'),parameter('da',p[2],'rad/s','Nonnegative angular dead-zone threshold.'),parameter('ba',p[3],'rad/s','Additive effective angular calibration bias.')], 'gf*v_f','ga*sign(v_a)*Max(Abs(v_a)-da, 0) + ba','0.5', KIN+' Dead-zone angular input-response model.',
 'Physical reasoning supplied the unicycle pose update. Preliminary steady-command residuals showed disproportionately small turns for small `Abs(v_a)`, and signed-linear, cubic and dead-zone maps were examined. The dead-zone operator `sign(v_a)*Max(Abs(v_a)-da, 0)` was chosen as a physically interpretable way to represent low-command inactivity. It was fitted directly, not returned by symbolic regression. A constant `ba` allows effective angular offset because the simple affine calibration improved aggregate fit.',
 '`da` removes turning commanded below a threshold before the angular bias is added. `ga*sign(v_a)*Max(Abs(v_a)-da, 0)` is the calibrated signed excess angular command. `ba` mathematically shifts angular response equally for positive and negative commands.',
 'The dead zone helps steady angular response, but does not explain command-change lag; its held-out position error does not beat the simpler midpoint model. Its shape near zero and the interpretation of the angular bias are weakly determined.',
 BASEMETHOD+'Starting values were `gf = 0.83`, `ga = 0.8`, `da = 0.08` rad/s, and `ba = 0` rad/s, rounded from preliminary steady-command linear/dead-zone fits. Bounds were `0.2 <= gf <= 1.5`, `0.1 <= ga <= 1.5`, `0 <= da <= 0.35` rad/s, and `-0.15 <= ba <= 0.15` rad/s. The nonnegative threshold and positive gains preserve the intended command signs apart from the separately fitted small bias.', 'fit_models.py')

p=R['phase']['parameters']
make('PhaseAdjusted',R['phase'],[parameter('gf',p[0],'-','Forward command gain.'),parameter('ga',p[1],'-','Linear angular command gain.'),parameter('ba',p[2],'rad/s','Additive angular calibration bias.'),parameter('alpha',p[3],'-','Fitted direction interpolation coefficient replacing the fixed midpoint coefficient.')], 'gf*v_f','ga*v_a + ba','alpha', KIN+' Generalized theta-method direction interpolation; a memoryless phase correction of an affine-angular unicycle.',
 'The large transition-phase residuals motivated checking a limited phase correction rather than inventing state-location terms. I replaced the fixed `0.5` interpolation coefficient with `alpha` in `phi + alpha*Ts*(ga*v_a + ba)`. This guessed correction lets the translation use an earlier or later effective direction. The heading still integrates the current command; no previous-command state or true delay was added. The one-step geometric estimate was close to midpoint, but multi-step fitting preferred an earlier direction, illustrating the different criteria.',
 '`alpha` shifts the direction used for translation: `alpha = 0` is current heading and `alpha = 0.5` is midpoint heading. It changes only the direction, not the magnitude of translation or the heading increment. `ba` is an effective constant angular calibration correction. A fitted early direction can compensate some aggregated lag error without representing its missing internal dynamics.',
 'A fitted direction shift is not an identified actuator delay and cannot retain the previous command at a switch. It can be confounded with angular gain and bias; its held-out error is slightly worse than fixed midpoint. It is a phenomenological effective pose model.',
 BASEMETHOD+'Starting values were `gf = 0.83`, `ga = 0.68`, `ba = 0` rad/s, and `alpha = 0`. The gains came from preliminary steady-command fits, zero bias expresses no initial drift assumption, and current-heading translation is a familiar Euler baseline. Bounds were `0.2 <= gf <= 1.5`, `0.1 <= ga <= 1.5`, `-0.15 <= ba <= 0.15` rad/s, and `-4 <= alpha <= 2`. The wider interpolation range deliberately tests modest phase extrapolation at the known sampling interval.', 'fit_models.py')

p=W['parameters'];right='sign(v_f + b*v_a)*Max(Abs(v_f + b*v_a)-dw, 0)';left='sign(v_f - b*v_a)*Max(Abs(v_f - b*v_a)-dw, 0)'
s=f'gf*(({right}) + ({left}))/2';r=f'ga*(({right}) - ({left}))/(2*b)'
make('WheelDeadzone',W,[parameter('gf',p[0],'-','Gain on the mean dead-zone-corrected wheel speed command.'),parameter('ga',p[1],'-','Gain on the dead-zone-corrected differential wheel speed command.'),parameter('b',p[2],'m','Effective half-track conversion between body angular command and wheel linear command; radians treated as dimensionless. Not independently measured geometry.'),parameter('dw',p[3],'m/s','Common linear wheel-command dead-zone threshold.')],s,r,'0.5', KIN+' Symmetric differential-drive wheel dead zones with separate effective forward and angular odometry calibration.',
 'I first tested body-level nonlinear command maps. Small angular-command inactivity and the forward affine offset motivated a distinct wheel-level hypothesis: transform body commands into `v_f + b*v_a` and `v_f - b*v_a`, apply identical signed dead zones to both, then reconstruct translation and turning. The wheel mixing comes from differential-drive geometry, while the shared threshold is an unverified stiction-like hypothesis. Separate forward and angular gains allow different effective odometry calibrations. The initial single-start least-squares fit stalled near a threshold kink, so deterministic multistart refinement was used.',
 f'`b` sets wheel-command mixing; `dw` removes the magnitude below threshold separately in each wheel command. The right corrected command is `{right}` and the left corrected command is `{left}`. Their scaled mean gives effective forward speed `{s}`; their scaled difference gives effective angular speed `{r}`. This couples forward and turning response even though the two wheel dead zones are identical.',
 'The fitted `b` is an effective mixing parameter, not a defensible estimate of the physical TurtleBot wheel separation; its held-out-fold values vary substantially. The common threshold, separate odometry gains, and switching locations are partly confounded. Wheel dynamics, asymmetry, battery effects and transient velocity are not determined. The marginal validation advantage over midpoint is too small to justify recommending its extra parameters.',
 'Deterministic bounded multistart derivative-free minimization with `scipy.optimize.minimize(method="Powell")`, followed by bounded nonlinear least-squares polishing with `scipy.optimize.least_squares`. Powell was chosen because the signed dead-zone maps produce threshold kinks where a single finite-difference least-squares start can stop prematurely. All four fitted parameters were estimated jointly in every attempt; the attempt with lowest rollout sum of squares supplies the final values. Nine starts used `gf = 0.83`, `ga = 0.8`, every combination of `b = 0.06`, `b = 0.115`, or `b = 0.2` m and `dw = 0.003`, `dw = 0.015`, or `dw = 0.03` m/s. These bracket small-to-large effective mixing scales and low thresholds; `b = 0.115` m is an analyst-selected middle start, not a measured dimension. Bounds were `0.2 <= gf <= 1.5`, `0.1 <= ga <= 1.5`, `0.04 <= b <= 0.3` m, and `0 <= dw <= 0.04` m/s. Powell settings were `xtol = 1e-9`, `ftol = 1e-10`, `maxiter = 300`; least-squares polishing used `ftol = 1e-11`, `xtol = 1e-11`, `gtol = 1e-11`, `max_nfev = 400`, linear loss and the default trust-region reflective method with numerical Jacobian. No random numbers or seed are used. The complete attempts and validation fits are saved in wheel_results.json.', 'refine_wheels.py')

p=C['sparse_clipped']['parameters']
make('SparseCommandMap',C['sparse_clipped'],[parameter('gf',p[0],'-','Forward gain outside the forward dead zone.'),parameter('ga',p[1],'-','Linear angular-command coefficient.'),parameter('gc',p[2],'s^2/rad^2','Cubic angular-command coefficient; radians are explicit in this coefficient unit.'),parameter('gv',p[3],'s/m','Coefficient of forward-command-dependent angular gain.'),parameter('ba',p[4],'rad/s','Constant angular calibration offset.'),parameter('df',p[5],'m/s','Forward dead-zone threshold replacing the negative affine forward intercept.')], 'gf*sign(v_f)*Max(Abs(v_f)-df, 0)','ga*v_a + gc*v_a**3 + gv*v_f*v_a + ba','0.5',
 KIN+' Sparse polynomial input-response map with a zero-preserving forward dead zone. Structure discovery uses sparse regression; final coefficients use rollout least squares. The sparse-regression approach is related to SINDy: Brunton, Proctor and Kutz, Discovering governing equations from data by sparse identification of nonlinear dynamical systems, PNAS (2016), cited from memory, not fetched; this implementation uses a command-response library rather than a state-derivative SINDy fit.',
 'The physical pose update was retained. I formed effective forward and angular velocities from measured increments, using measured midpoint heading for body projection, then averaged phases 2 through 4 within each complete command block to reduce switch-lag contamination and avoid treating correlated samples as independent. A standardized candidate library contained `v_f`, `v_a`, `v_f**2`, `v_a**2`, `v_f*v_a`, `v_a**3`, `v_f*v_a**2`, and `sign(v_a)`, with an intercept. BIC-selected sparse regression retained only `v_f` for forward response and `v_a`, `v_f*v_a`, and `v_a**3` for angular response, plus intercepts. After an affine forward refit gave a negative intercept, I replaced it by a signed forward dead zone to preserve zero motion at zero forward command, then refitted all final coefficients. No spatial terms were searched or included.',
 '`gc*v_a**3` changes angular gain with squared angular-command magnitude. `gv*v_f*v_a` changes angular gain linearly with forward command. These are empirical mathematical corrections, not separately identified mechanical forces. `ba` shifts the angular map, while `df` suppresses very small forward commands. The final negative cubic coefficient contrasts with the preliminary steady-block coefficient, showing that rollout fitting also absorbs missing transient effects.',
 'The additional angular terms reduce training criterion but do not improve held-out position error; the speed-coupling coefficient varies considerably between folds. Cubic extrapolation can reverse angular response at sufficiently large commands, so this candidate is restricted to the observed range. The sparse-selected support is a hypothesis, not evidence that a unique nonlinear law has been recovered.',
 'Structure selection first used `sklearn.linear_model.LassoLarsIC(criterion="bic")` on 87 complete command blocks from `data/train.csv`: 29 blocks per trajectory with three steady-phase transitions per block. Each library column was divided by its training standard deviation. Least-angle Lasso with BIC needs no parameter starting values and was chosen as a small-library sparse baseline; it is not genetic-programming symbolic regression. Preliminary selection is reproduced by `residual_analysis.py`. All six final fitted parameters were then estimated together with bounded nonlinear rollout least squares using `scipy.optimize.least_squares` (default trust-region reflective method, numerical Jacobian, linear loss, `ftol = 1e-10`, `xtol = 1e-10`, `gtol = 1e-10`, `max_nfev = 400`). Final starting values were `gf = 0.9`, `ga = 0.68`, `gc = -0.06` s^2/rad^2, `gv = 0.41` s/m, `ba = 0.0075` rad/s, and `df = 0.008` m/s, rounded from the earlier sparse-support affine rollout fit in `fit_extensions.py`; none of its coefficients were held fixed. Bounds were `0.2 <= gf <= 1.5`, `0.1 <= ga <= 1.5`, `-0.4 <= gc <= 1` s^2/rad^2, `-2 <= gv <= 2` s/m, `-0.15 <= ba <= 0.15` rad/s, and `0 <= df <= 0.06` m/s, allowing either sign of nonlinear correction while retaining positive baseline gains. No stochastic search or random seed is used.', 'fit_clipped.py')

pool={'order':['CalibratedMidpoint','WheelDeadzone','ForwardDeadzone','AngularDeadzone','PhaseAdjusted','SparseCommandMap'],'recommended':'CalibratedMidpoint','reason':'Recommend the two-gain physical midpoint model: its leave-one-trajectory-out 25-transition (5 s) `x` RMSE is 0.029936 m. The wheel-dead-zone model achieves 0.029800 m, only about 0.000136 m lower, while adding two weakly identifiable parameters; this is not convincing evidence of improvement with three validation trajectories. Other structural candidates give about 0.0306 to 0.0315 m. The recommended model preserves zero-command stationarity and coordinate invariance, has no polynomial extrapolation, and provides the clearest defensible memoryless approximation. Selection uses training-only validation, not inaccessible test results.'}
Path('models/pool.json').write_text(json.dumps(pool,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Wrote',len(pool['order']),'candidates')
